{"name":"White Metal","hex":"#d1d1cf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white metal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tin- or lead-based alloy (commonly used to make bearings, jewellery, toy soldiers, fusible plugs, some medals and metal type).","Silver that cannot be labeled as silver in the UK because it does not bear an Assay Office hallmark."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_metal"}],"entries":[{"title":"White metal","blocks":[{"text":"The white metals are a series of often decorative bright metal alloys used as a base for plated silverware, ornaments or novelties, as well as any of several lead-based or tin-based alloys used for things like bearings, jewellery, miniature figures, fusible plugs, some medals and metal type. The term is also used in the antiques trade for an item suspected of being silver, but not hallmarked."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_metal","wikidata":"Q910530"},{"title":"Metal","blocks":[{"text":"A metal is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile and malleable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal","wikidata":"Q11426"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pigs_and_Bars_of_Babbitt.jpg/500px-Pigs_and_Bars_of_Babbitt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pigs_and_Bars_of_Babbitt.jpg/500px-Pigs_and_Bars_of_Babbitt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bars and ingots of Babbitt metal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APigs_and_Bars_of_Babbitt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Sauce_boat.jpg/500px-Sauce_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Sauce_boat.jpg/1280px-Sauce_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Sauce_boat.jpg/500px-Sauce_boat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Metal in the form of a gravy boat made from stainless steel, an alloy largely composed of iron, chromium and nickel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASauce_boat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Gallium_crystals.jpg/500px-Gallium_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Gallium_crystals.jpg/500px-Gallium_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gallium crystals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGallium_crystals.jp